PROTECTION OF WOMEN VICTIMS OF VIOLENCE

Part One

General Article 4 The Regional Government is responsible for efforts to protect women from acts of violence that occur in public or private life in the form of:
a. physical violence;
b. sexual violence;
c. economic violence;
d. social violence;
e. psychological violence;
f. domestic neglect;
g. coercion or deprivation of liberty; and h. threats of certain actions.

Article 5 Every person who hears, sees or knows of the occurrence of Violence as referred to in Article 4 shall make efforts to:
a. prevent the continuation of criminal acts;
b. provide protection to victims;
c. provide emergency assistance; and d. assist in the complaint process and submission of applications for protection orders.

Article 2

Sufficiently clear Article 3 Sufficiently clear Article 4 Letter a What is meant by "physical violence" is any act that results in pain, injury, wounds or disability to a person's body, miscarriage, unconsciousness and or causes death.

Letter b What is meant by "sexual violence" is any act in the form of sexual harassment, forced sexual intercourse, forced unnatural or unwanted sexual intercourse, forced sexual intercourse with others for commercial purposes and or certain purposes.

Letter c What is meant by "economic violence" is an act that restricts a person from working inside or outside the home to produce money and goods, including allowing a working person to be exploited, while someone does not fulfill the economic needs of the family.

Letter d What is meant by "social violence" is an act by a person or group of people that causes injury or loss of life to a person or may cause physical damage or damage to another person's property.

Letter e What is meant by "psychological violence" is an act that results in fear, loss of self-confidence, loss of ability to act, feelings of helplessness and or severe psychological suffering to a person.

Letter f What is meant by "Domestic Neglect" is: a. an act that results in the non-fulfillment of;
b. the needs of children in a proper manner, whether physical, mental, spiritual or social;
c. an act of deliberately neglecting to maintain, care for, or take care of a child as appropriate;
d. an act of neglecting a person within the scope of one's household, whereas according to the law applicable to him or her or due to an agreement or contract he or she is obliged to provide livelihood, care, or maintenance to that person;
e. an act that results in economic dependence by restricting and/or prohibiting decent work inside or outside the home so that the victim is under the control of that person.

Letter g Sufficiently clear Letter h What is meant by "threat of certain action" is any unlawful act in the form of speech, writing, images, symbols, or body movements, whether with or without using means that cause fear or restrict a person's fundamental freedom.

Bagian Kedua

Special Protection of Children Article 19 (1) Special protection of children shall be provided to:
a. children in emergency situations;
b. children in conflict with the law;
c. children from minority and isolated groups;
d. children who are exploited economically and j or sexually;
e. children who are victims of narcotics, alcohol, psychotropic, and other addictive substance abuse;
f. children who are victims of pornography;
g. children with HIV/ AIDS;
h. child victims of kidnapping, sale, and Zor trafficking;
1. child victims of physical and / or psychological Violence;

J. child victims of sexual crimes;
k. child victims of terrorist networks;
1. children with disabilities: m. child victims of maltreatment and neglect;
n. children with deviant social behavior; and o. children who become victims of stigmatization from labeling related to their parents' condition.

.J (2) The Regional Government in implementing the handling of special protection of children as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations.

Bagian Kedua

Obligations and Responsibilities Article 24 Obligations and responsibilities in providing protection to (1) women and children from acts of violence shall be the joint responsibility of the Regional Government and the Community.

The obligations and responsibilities of the Regional Government as referred to in (2) paragraph (1). include: . a. establishing, implementing policies, programs, and conducting cooperation~ activities in the implementation of protection of women and children from acts of violence;
b. facilitating the establishment of institutional protection of women and children from acts of violence and providing support for facilities and infrastructure; c. allocating budget for the implementation of protection of women and children from acts of violence according to the financial capacity of the Region; d. fostering and supervising the implementation of protection of women and children from acts of violence;
e. providing protection services for women and children victims of violence;
f. encouraging and increasing community participation; and g. appointing parents and/ or substitute family caregivers as a protection measure for children who are victims of violence.

Paragraph 1

