b. handling of child victims of acts of violence; and c. rehabilitation of child victims of acts of violence.

Part Three

Integrated Services for Victims of Acts of Violence Article 8 (1) The Regional Government shall implement integrated services for victims of acts of violence, through activities:
a. complaint services;
b. health services;
c. Counseling;
d. psychological services;
e. spiritual guidance;
f. social rehabilitation services;
g. legal assistance and support services;
h. safe house services; and y or i. repatriation and social reintegration.
2. In the event that the Regional Government does not yet have counseling personnel as referred to in paragraph (1) letter c, it may request assistance from social workers, support volunteers, and/or spiritual counselors.
3. Services as referred to in paragraph (1) must be supported by facilities in the form of: a. special service rooms within the police force;
b. expert and professional personnel;
c. service centers and Safe Houses; and/or d. other facilities and infrastructure necessary for the recovery of victims including perpetrators.
4. The provision of services to victims of acts of violence shall be carried out in an integrated manner by the Regional Government together with other social institutions. (5) Other social institutions as referred to in paragraph (4) may receive and send case referrals from other service units in a networked manner. (6) Integrated services for victims of acts of violence as referred to in paragraphs (1) through (5) shall be implemented in accordance with standard operating procedures.
7. Provisions regarding standard operating procedures as referred to in paragraph (6) shall be regulated in a Regent Regulation.

Article 9 (1) Complaint services as referred to in Article 8 paragraph (1) letter a shall be implemented to receive reports of acts of violence.

2. Reports as referred to in paragraph (1) may originate from: a. victims;
b. referrals; or c. outreach.

Article 10

1. Health services as referred to in Article 8 paragraph - (1) letter b, shall be implemented using health facilities owned by the Regional Government or other referral hospitals by providing medical treatment and health recovery services to victims.
2. Health services as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be performed by health personnel to victims in accordance with professional standards, standard operating procedures and the medical needs of victims.
3. Health services as referred to in paragraph (1), through efforts: a. anamnesis to victims;
b. examination of victims;
c. providing first aid;
d. health recovery both physical and psychological, conducting efforts to prevent disease and other health disorders;
e. counseling;
f. referring to more adequate health service facilities if necessary may refer to other parties with a multidisciplinary approach, including health services, medicolegal, psychosocial support and legal assistance; and/or g. providing information to the police.
4. In addition to the efforts as referred to in paragraph (3) for certain cases, health personnel may provide reproductive health services in accordance with medical needs.
5. In providing services as referred to in paragraphs (3) and (4), health personnel must create medical records in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations, (6) For every medical action to be performed by health personnel there must be consent for medical action from the victim or their family in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations.
7. For investigation purposes, authorized health personnel must prepare a visum et repertum and/or visum et repertum psichiatricum. or prepare a medical certificate.
8. Health personnel have the right to obtain legal protection.

Article 11 Legal assistance and protection services as referred to in Article 8 paragraph (1) letter g include:
a. providing protection in a Safe House;
b. providing legal information to victims;
c. providing support for victims as witnesses from the investigation process to the verdict;
d. providing special legal protection for child victims of acts of violence may be carried out by appointing guardianship in accordance with laws and regulations.

Article 12 (1) Repatriation and social reintegration as referred to in Article 8 paragraph (1) letter g shall be implemented with the following principles:
a. voluntary;
b. safe and dignified; and c. respect for rights.
2. In the event that the Regional Government is not yet able to implement repatriation and social reintegration as referred to in paragraph (1), it may request assistance from related parties.
3. Social services to provide social welfare for children in conflict with the law shall be carried out by the Regional Government together with LPKS with other social institutions.
4. LPKS and other social institutions as referred to in paragraph (3) are rehabilitation and temporary shelter places for children in conflict with the law before repatriation and social reintegration are implemented.

CHAPTER II

RIGHTS OF WOMEN AND CHILD VICTIMS OF VIOLENCE

Article 2 Women and child victims of violence receive rights, including: a. the right to be respected for their dignity and worth as human beings;
b. the right to recovery;
c. the right to determine their own decisions;
d. the right to obtain information;
e. the right to confidentiality;
f. the right to social rehabilitation;
g. the right to complaint handling that is fast, appropriate, comfortable and according to needs;
h. the right of Victims and their families to obtain facilities in the judicial process;
i. the right to Assistance;

j. the right to security; and
k. the right to restitution.

Article 3 Child victims of violence in addition to receiving rights as referred to in Article 4, also receive special rights, including:
a. the right to survival, growth and development;

b. the right to basic population services;
c. the right to equal protection;
d. the right to be free from various stigma; and e. the right to obtain freedom.

