PROTECTION OF WOMEN VICTIMS OF VIOLENCE

Part One

General Article 4 The Regional Government is responsible for efforts to protect women from acts of violence that occur in public or private life in the form of:
a. physical violence;
b. sexual violence;
c. economic violence;
d. social violence;
e. psychological violence;
f. domestic neglect;
g. coercion or deprivation of liberty; and h. threats of certain actions.

Article 5 Every person who hears, sees or knows of the occurrence of Violence as referred to in Article 4 shall make efforts to:
a. prevent the continuation of criminal acts;
b. provide protection to victims;
c. provide emergency assistance; and d. assist in the complaint process and submission of applications for protection orders.

Part Two

Prevention of Acts of Violence Article 16 (1) The Regional Government shall prevent acts of violence against women by:
a. socializing laws and regulations;
b. conducting empowerment and awareness raising to families, parents and the community by providing information, guidance and/or counseling; c. conducting efforts to increase the quantity and quality of education both formal and non-formal and informal;
d. conducting efforts to build community participation and concern to implement prevention and protection of women and children from violence in an integrated community-based manner;
e. building networks and cooperation with law enforcement apparatus, government apparatus, universities, religious institutions/organizations and various non-governmental organizations in efforts to prevent violence against women and children;
f. establishing a violence prevention system, mapping locations or areas prone to violence;
g. increasing community awareness that respects women and protects children;
h. establishing UPTD PPA in every Sub-district, and Women and Children Protection Service Posts at Village and Sub-district levels; and i. establishing a women and children protection task force.
2. In addition to the Regional Government, prevention as referred to in paragraph (1) must be implemented by: a. family and/or closest relatives;
b. community;
c. vertical agencies;
d. law enforcement apparatus: e. educational institutions;
f. health institutions;
g. religious institutions/organizations;
h. social community institutions;
1. business sector; and j. other institutions.
3. Prevention of acts of violence against women as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be carried out in an integrated manner and coordinated by the Regional Apparatus having the main duties and functions in the field of women's empowerment and child protection.

Paragraf 2

Prevention of Violence Against Children Article 15 (1) Prevention of acts of violence against children as referred to in Article 14 letter a shall be implemented by:
a. establishing a network in efforts to prevent violence against children; b. conducting coordination, integration, synchronization of violence prevention based on partnership patterns with the community, private sector and non-governmental organizations; and c. conducting socialization of laws and regulations related to the protection of child victims of violence.
2. Prevention of violence against children as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be carried out in an integrated manner and coordinated by the Regional Apparatus having the main duties and functions in the field of women's empowerment and child protection.

Article 16 (1) Prevention of acts of violence against children in addition to being implemented by the Regional Apparatus as referred to in Article 14 paragraph (2), also involves the parties: a. parents, family and / or closest relatives;
b. community;
c. vertical agencies;
d. law enforcement apparatus;
e. educational institutions;
f. health institutions;
g. religious institutions / organizations; h. social community institutions;
1. child social welfare institutions;

J. business sector; and
k. other institutions engaged in the field of child protection care. (2) Provisions regarding the involvement of parties as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be regulated in a Regent Regulation.

